["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"AI Didn&#x27;t Collapse Because a Phase Transition Happened, Not Because of More Parameters Physicist Nigel Goldenfeld uses the renormalization group and phase transitions to explain the mechanism by which AI still hasn&#x27;t collapsed at a trillion parameters, and applies the same framework to answer why the genetic code is unique. Into the Impossible With Brian Keating Into the Impossible With Brian Keating The Physics Reason AI Works When It Shouldn’t Phase transitions Renormalization group AI interpretability Overfitting Genetic code Life as physics renormalization group critical exponent data collapse horizontal gene transfer ridge regression\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-intotheimpossi-ai-有效是因为它发生了相变/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7133e1b0fe88d6bc-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">1:17:53</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Into the Impossible With Brian Keating</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>AI Didn&#x27;t Collapse Because a Phase Transition Happened, Not Because of More Parameters</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Physicist Nigel Goldenfeld uses the renormalization group and phase transitions to explain the mechanism by which AI still hasn&#x27;t collapsed at a trillion parameters, and applies the same framework to answer why the genetic code is unique.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Phase transitions</span><span class=\"tag\">Renormalization group</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"OpenAI&#x27;s Own Chip Is 100x Ahead? That&#x27;s a Self-Chosen Benchmark on a Dedicated Stack OpenAI&#x27;s first in-house chip, Jalapeno, beats the GB200 by 100x on a benchmark OpenAI picked — but that result comes from a dedicated chip running dedicated models on a dedicated software stack; the real moat is system integration and pricing power. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato First Benchmark Results on an OpenAI GPU Chip design OpenAI Benchmarking Inference performance Custom AI silicon Business strategy speculative decoding multi-token prediction (MTP) time to last token (TTLT) MXFP4 scale-up / scale-out domain Tomahawk 6\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-techtechpotato-自研芯片百倍领先-基准选择与商业筹码才是关键/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/fba1bec187512ffb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">31:10</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>OpenAI&#x27;s Own Chip Is 100x Ahead? That&#x27;s a Self-Chosen Benchmark on a Dedicated Stack</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">OpenAI&#x27;s first in-house chip, Jalapeno, beats the GB200 by 100x on a benchmark OpenAI picked — but that result comes from a dedicated chip running dedicated models on a dedicated software stack; the real moat is system integration and pricing power.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Chip design</span><span class=\"tag\">OpenAI</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"48 Observations from the Robot Conference: Nobody Is Making Money, Yet 18 Companies Made 36 Investments 48 observations from the 2026 World Robot Conference: robots turning into assembly kits, real demos finally showing up, modifier inflation. Nobody in the industry is making money, yet 18 companies made 36 investments, and the cost of a robot body has fallen tenfold in three years. 脑放电波 脑放电波 具身智能的“山寨机时代”：亲历2026世界机器人大会，我们的48个观察-Vol 104 Embodied AI Humanoid robots World Robot Conference Falling costs Investment ecosystem Knockoff-phone era VLA (vision-language-action model) ODM (original design manufacturer) UWB (ultra-wideband) Zero-shot\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-cn768a8a-具身智能的山寨机时代-没人赚钱但18家互投/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ac5fc3c22c8141af-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"脑\"><span class=\"dur\">51:04</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">脑放电波</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>48 Observations from the Robot Conference: Nobody Is Making Money, Yet 18 Companies Made 36 Investments</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">48 observations from the 2026 World Robot Conference: robots turning into assembly kits, real demos finally showing up, modifier inflation. Nobody in the industry is making money, yet 18 companies made 36 investments, and the cost of a robot body has fallen tenfold in three years.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Embodied AI</span><span class=\"tag\">Humanoid robots</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Compute is concentrating into two labs several times over each year, and by 2028 they will control most of the world&#x27;s compute Compute is concentrating into Anthropic and OpenAI at a rate of several times a year; by 2028 they will control most of the world&#x27;s effective compute, may push the price of compute to $50 million per megawatt, and could trigger a sovereign debt crisis. Dwarkesh Podcast Dwarkesh Dylan Patel – Anthropic &amp; OpenAI will have most of the world’s compute by 2028 Compute Lab economics Capital expenditure Centralization Sovereign debt China chips FLOP RSI hyperscaler crowding-out effect continual learning\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-dwarkesh-2028-年前两家实验室将控制全球大部分算力/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/59e51ac2f0c2b285-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">1:16:53</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Dwarkesh Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Compute is concentrating into two labs several times over each year, and by 2028 they will control most of the world&#x27;s compute</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Compute is concentrating into Anthropic and OpenAI at a rate of several times a year; by 2028 they will control most of the world&#x27;s effective compute, may push the price of compute to $50 million per megawatt, and could trigger a sovereign debt crisis.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Compute</span><span class=\"tag\">Lab economics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Rack Radiation Over the Limit: Two Rounds of Debugging Failed, and the Fix Was Hiding at an Unrelated Conference Radiation at 26.565GHz off the back of the rack came in over the limit. Two rounds of troubleshooting turned up nothing, and the fix finally came from a paper presented at an unrelated conference: add absorber material at the connector. This is not black magic, it is high-frequency physics. Oxide and Friends Oxide and Friends The Return to the Chamber of Mysteries EMC Hardware engineering Rack servers Optical transceivers Compliance testing EMC (electromagnetic compatibility) Slot antenna Grounded coplanar waveguide QSFP cage Line (EMC chamber)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-oxide-emc-合规是玄学吗-一次机架级辐射超标排障记/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/6b5eb6a03ee5e8af-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">1:16:08</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Oxide and Friends</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Rack Radiation Over the Limit: Two Rounds of Debugging Failed, and the Fix Was Hiding at an Unrelated Conference</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Radiation at 26.565GHz off the back of the rack came in over the limit. Two rounds of troubleshooting turned up nothing, and the fix finally came from a paper presented at an unrelated conference: add absorber material at the connector. This is not black magic, it is high-frequency physics.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">EMC</span><span class=\"tag\">Hardware engineering</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Turns Software From an Engineering Constraint Into a Capital One Martin Casado and Steven Sinofsky&#x27;s central claim: AI has moved software from an engineering constraint to a capital constraint — a team of 20 can now effectively spend a billion dollars, which is why startups can compete head-on with the incumbents. The a16z Show a16z The New Economics of AI | Martin Casado &amp; Steven Sinofsky Compute Capital Startups Venture Capital AI Economics Incumbent Competition Foom / fast takeoff TAM / Total Addressable Market in-distribution Mythical man-month expert systems\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-a16z-ai-正在把工程问题变成资本问题/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7b2f032532c3a865-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:03:32</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The a16z Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>AI Turns Software From an Engineering Constraint Into a Capital One</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Martin Casado and Steven Sinofsky&#x27;s central claim: AI has moved software from an engineering constraint to a capital constraint — a team of 20 can now effectively spend a billion dollars, which is why startups can compete head-on with the incumbents.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Compute</span><span class=\"tag\">Capital</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Shakespeare&#x27;s History Plays Are Tudor Propaganda Shakespeare&#x27;s history plays are full of historical distortions, essentially political propaganda for the Tudor dynasty: vilifying Richard III, avoiding sensitive topics about Henry VIII, to legitimize their rule. 忽左忽右 忽左忽右 495 陆大鹏谈莎士比亚戏剧与英国历史的互相影响 Shakespeare English History Tudor Dynasty Political Propaganda English Literature Theater intertextuality\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-cn2aa250-莎士比亚历史剧是都铎王朝的宣传工具/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/cb6acd126210dffb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"忽\"><span class=\"dur\">1:16:41</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">忽左忽右</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Shakespeare&#x27;s History Plays Are Tudor Propaganda</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Shakespeare&#x27;s history plays are full of historical distortions, essentially political propaganda for the Tudor dynasty: vilifying Richard III, avoiding sensitive topics about Henry VIII, to legitimize their rule.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Shakespeare</span><span class=\"tag\">English History</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Administrative Humiliation Is Why the Poor Hate Government: Applying for Benefits Is a Time Tax Administrative burden in government benefit programs is not an efficiency problem but an instrument of social control: it punishes the poor with humiliation and paperwork, turns progressive policy into a funnel that screens out all but a few, and ends by making recipients hate the government. The Ezra Klein Show Ezra Klein Show This Is Why People Hate the Government Welfare state Administrative burden Time tax Medicaid Tax reform American politics time tax ordeal mechanism improper payment only-once policy\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-ezra-时间税-行政羞辱是穷人恨政府的原因/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a8482192037a6711-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:17:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Ezra Klein Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Administrative Humiliation Is Why the Poor Hate Government: Applying for Benefits Is a Time Tax</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Administrative burden in government benefit programs is not an efficiency problem but an instrument of social control: it punishes the poor with humiliation and paperwork, turns progressive policy into a funnel that screens out all but a few, and ends by making recipients hate the government.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Welfare state</span><span class=\"tag\">Administrative burden</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Human Click Data Is a Bug: Agent Search Has to Rebuild Indexing and Pricing When search&#x27;s customer stops being a human and becomes an agent, click data stops working, and the index, the pricing and the business model all have to be built again; Parag offers a new framework built on a quality/cost/latency triangle and on Shapley values. Training Data Training Data Parallel’s Parag Agrawal: Building a New Web for AI Agents Agent search Web economics Shapley value Google Cloud Data pricing Infrastructure Shapley value grounding fresh index memory hierarchy\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-trainingdata-人类点击数据是-bug-agent-搜索重建网络经济/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/bca134f7cdc5e5aa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">55:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Training Data</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Human Click Data Is a Bug: Agent Search Has to Rebuild Indexing and Pricing</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">When search&#x27;s customer stops being a human and becomes an agent, click data stops working, and the index, the pricing and the business model all have to be built again; Parag offers a new framework built on a quality/cost/latency triangle and on Shapley values.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Agent search</span><span class=\"tag\">Web economics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Background agents will eat 90% of compute, so inference should trade latency for throughput The next wave of AI inference is not chat but background agents, and they will eat 90% of compute. Sale Research therefore optimizes for throughput rather than latency, and will even accept 80% availability, in exchange for prices that fall by orders of magnitude — and it argues Nvidia&#x27;s performance-per-watt advantage is overrated. Invest Like the Best Invest Like the Best Neil Movva - Making AI 10x Cheaper - [Invest Like the Best, EP.488] AI Inference GPUs Data Centers Open Models Agents Compute Cost KV cache HBM (high-bandwidth memory) SRAM (static random-access memory) Tensor Core Harness\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-ilt-推理成本需降3-6个数量级-后台任务才是主战场/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/32e8a839c878b0c2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">1:18:07</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Invest Like the Best</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Background agents will eat 90% of compute, so inference should trade latency for throughput</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The next wave of AI inference is not chat but background agents, and they will eat 90% of compute. Sale Research therefore optimizes for throughput rather than latency, and will even accept 80% availability, in exchange for prices that fall by orders of magnitude — and it argues Nvidia&#x27;s performance-per-watt advantage is overrated.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI Inference</span><span class=\"tag\">GPUs</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\" data-hay=\"There Is No Formula for Getting In: The Anti-Anxiety Truth About U.S. College Admissions Low admit rates are a story about roughly 80 selective schools nationally; for most families there is no formula, and the most useful thing a parent can do is hold a standing meeting and control what is controllable. Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens Ask Lisa 286: ENCORE: Getting into College with Rick Clark US college admissions Admit rates Application essays Test-optional Athletic scholarships Parent-teen relationship holistic review test-optional Common App FAFSA net price calculator\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-asklisa-申请没有万能配方-美国大学录取的反焦虑真相/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ace27132dfc1e3aa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">48:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>There Is No Formula for Getting In: The Anti-Anxiety Truth About U.S. College Admissions</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Low admit rates are a story about roughly 80 selective schools nationally; for most families there is no formula, and the most useful thing a parent can do is hold a standing meeting and control what is controllable.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">US college admissions</span><span class=\"tag\">Admit rates</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"No Atrocities in the SS Officer&#x27;s Album — Only Blueberries and Holidays A private photo album kept by an SS officer records not atrocities but holidays, dinners and blueberry picking; it proves the men who carried out the killing knew exactly what they were doing, and it is a reminder that understanding the Holocaust means looking at the perpetrators. Witness History Witness History Auschwitz: An SS officer’s photo album Holocaust Nazis Photo album Perpetrator studies Auschwitz Historical evidence Solahütte Counterintelligence Corps Frankfurt Auschwitz Trials\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-witness-纳粹屠夫的私人相册-没有惨状只有蓝莓和假期/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4ad7b76f4c635f2c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">10:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>No Atrocities in the SS Officer&#x27;s Album — Only Blueberries and Holidays</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A private photo album kept by an SS officer records not atrocities but holidays, dinners and blueberry picking; it proves the men who carried out the killing knew exactly what they were doing, and it is a reminder that understanding the Holocaust means looking at the perpetrators.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Holocaust</span><span class=\"tag\">Nazis</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"Twenty Thousand Robots Shipped, Nine in Ten Idle: Embodied AI Enters Its ROI Era Beneath the heat of WRC, there may be only one or two startups genuinely doing pre-training, real deployment demand may be only 10% of shipments, and the industry has moved on from demo theater into a hands-on phase where ROI has to be calculated. 卫诗婕｜漫谈Light the Star 漫谈Light the Star 84.49 家央企采买进场，史上最热WRC：泡沫被挤压，具身行业进入实战阶段｜14 年从业者的行家鉴别指南 Embodied AI Humanoid Robots WRC World Models Data Pipeline Robotics Investment VLA / Vision-Language-Action model World Model Teleoperation EMG / Electromyography signals Data Pipeline Post-training\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-lightstar-两万台出货九成吃灰-具身行业进入算roi的实战期/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/adbddf5c93cfc63c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"卫\"><span class=\"dur\">1:02:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">卫诗婕｜漫谈Light the Star</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Twenty Thousand Robots Shipped, Nine in Ten Idle: Embodied AI Enters Its ROI Era</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Beneath the heat of WRC, there may be only one or two startups genuinely doing pre-training, real deployment demand may be only 10% of shipments, and the industry has moved on from demo theater into a hands-on phase where ROI has to be calculated.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Embodied AI</span><span class=\"tag\">Humanoid Robots</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Intel Drops EMIB for UCIe: 1.2GB of Cache Behind 256 Cores Diamond Rapids is expected in 2027 with up to 256 P-cores, 1.2GB of last-level cache and 16 channels of DDR5; it replaces EMIB with trimmable compute building blocks and a UCIe interconnect, and the huge cache is its answer to the AI era. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato How Intel&#x27;s New CPU Finally Makes Sense CPU architecture Data centers Intel Xeon Chiplets Last-level cache AI infrastructure Fan-out fabric architecture Compute building block Fabric hub UCIe Foveros 3D Direct\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-techtechpotato-下一代-xeon-把宝押在-1-2gb-末级缓存上/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/fc7c90dc34e0b3f0-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">21:44</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Intel Drops EMIB for UCIe: 1.2GB of Cache Behind 256 Cores</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Diamond Rapids is expected in 2027 with up to 256 P-cores, 1.2GB of last-level cache and 16 channels of DDR5; it replaces EMIB with trimmable compute building blocks and a UCIe interconnect, and the huge cache is its answer to the AI era.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">CPU architecture</span><span class=\"tag\">Data centers</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"95% of AI Agent Projects Die in Pilot: What&#x27;s Missing Isn&#x27;t Intelligence, It&#x27;s a Control Layer AI has moved from generating content to acting on its own. 95% of agent projects are stuck in pilot and never reach production, because what&#x27;s missing isn&#x27;t intelligence but a runtime control layer. Eye On A.I. Eye On A.I. 95% of AI Agent Projects Fail to Reach Production. Here&#x27;s Why | Manoj Saxena, TrustWise Agents AI governance Enterprise software Runtime control Token cost runtime governance AI control tower guardian agents semantic action layer trust posture management\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-eyeonai-智能体流量首超人类-ai-失控才是落地最大瓶颈/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b277eb062496b5a5-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:49</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Eye On A.I.</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>95% of AI Agent Projects Die in Pilot: What&#x27;s Missing Isn&#x27;t Intelligence, It&#x27;s a Control Layer</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI has moved from generating content to acting on its own. 95% of agent projects are stuck in pilot and never reach production, because what&#x27;s missing isn&#x27;t intelligence but a runtime control layer.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Agents</span><span class=\"tag\">AI governance</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Photonics&#x27; Third Wave: Out of the Lab in Five Years, Conquering Drones First Photons cannot interact like electrons, but wavelength multiplexing enables massive parallelism and ultra-low latency. All-optical neural networks could land within five years, with the first stop not servers but drones and real-time control. Brains and Machines Brains and Machines Will Photonics Loosen the Bottlenecks in Neural Systems? photonics neuromorphic low latency WDM SOA WDM (Wavelength Division Multiplexing) Semiconductor Optical Amplifier (SOA) McCulloch-Pitts neuron model Indium Phosphide (InP) Femtojoule per operation TDM (Time Division Multiplexing)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-brainsandmachi-光子计算第三次浪潮-五年内走出实验室-先征服无人机/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/9151fd6dae1293cb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"B\"><span class=\"dur\">45:49</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Brains and Machines</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Photonics&#x27; Third Wave: Out of the Lab in Five Years, Conquering Drones First</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Photons cannot interact like electrons, but wavelength multiplexing enables massive parallelism and ultra-low latency. All-optical neural networks could land within five years, with the first stop not servers but drones and real-time control.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">photonics</span><span class=\"tag\">neuromorphic</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The Solid State Transformer Has Been Hyped for Fifty Years; AI Data Centers Are Its First Real Buyer The solid state transformer failed three times over — in the navy, in high-speed rail, in the smart grid — because all it offered was ‘smaller’. The copper problem and the 800-volt DC problem inside AI data centers are the first reasons it has ever had to exist. Asianometry Asianometry Has the Solid State Transformer’s Time Finally Come? Power electronics Data center power Solid state transformers 800V DC Silicon carbide Startup funding Solid State Transformer (SST) IGBT Silicon Carbide (SiC) Galvanic isolation 800VDC architecture PETT\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-asianometry-固态变压器吹了五十年-ai数据中心是第一个真买家/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a4d5049243d5f237-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Asianometry</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>The Solid State Transformer Has Been Hyped for Fifty Years; AI Data Centers Are Its First Real Buyer</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The solid state transformer failed three times over — in the navy, in high-speed rail, in the smart grid — because all it offered was ‘smaller’. The copper problem and the 800-volt DC problem inside AI data centers are the first reasons it has ever had to exist.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Power electronics</span><span class=\"tag\">Data center power</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"A Pitch Tells Two Stories: The Project, And The Buyer&#x27;s Promotion Hollywood screenwriter Rhett Reese on what a pitch actually is: the project story runs on the surface, the story of the other person&#x27;s success runs underneath. Make them the protagonist, lower their risk, enlarge their reward, and your pitch lands far more often. Think Fast Talk Smart Think Fast Talk Smart 317. Plot Twist: The Second Story Hiding Inside Every Great Pitch Pitching Communication Hollywood AI Collaboration pitch gatekeeper auteur\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-thinkfast-好提案藏着两个故事-你的故事和对方升职的故事/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7fd7a03d438b4365-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">24:09</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Think Fast Talk Smart</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>A Pitch Tells Two Stories: The Project, And The Buyer&#x27;s Promotion</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Hollywood screenwriter Rhett Reese on what a pitch actually is: the project story runs on the surface, the story of the other person&#x27;s success runs underneath. Make them the protagonist, lower their risk, enlarge their reward, and your pitch lands far more often.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Pitching</span><span class=\"tag\">Communication</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Black Holes Have Only Two Parameters, but How They Form Is Still a Mystery Gravitational waves and the EHT confirm that black holes really do have only two parameters, but how a 120-solar-mass black hole forms, whether dark matter is made of tiny black holes, and how the last parsec gets crossed all remain unsolved. Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape Mindscape 365 | Vitor Cardoso on Why Black Holes Are Special Black holes Gravitational waves Event Horizon Telescope Dark matter Quantum gravity LISA no-hair theorem Kerr metric light ring cosmic censorship matched filtering last parsec problem\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-mindscape-黑洞只有两个参数-形成机制却仍是谜/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d69097efcbd03611-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">1:05:36</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Black Holes Have Only Two Parameters, but How They Form Is Still a Mystery</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Gravitational waves and the EHT confirm that black holes really do have only two parameters, but how a 120-solar-mass black hole forms, whether dark matter is made of tiny black holes, and how the last parsec gets crossed all remain unsolved.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Black holes</span><span class=\"tag\">Gravitational waves</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"The UFO boom is not an astronomy question, it is a question of belief When the evidence stops at testimony, whether the universe is crowded becomes a matter of faith. The further science advances, the rarer extraterrestrial life looks in the observations; when we talk about UFOs, what we are really talking about is ourselves. EconTalk EconTalk Are We Alone? Cosmic Loneliness and the Longing to Believe (with Adam Kirsch) UFOs Extraterrestrial life Philosophy of science Anti-expert sentiment Social psychology UAP euphemism treadmill book experience Drake equation AATIP\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-econtalk-所有外星人想象都是人类的自画像/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f8da698e33560635-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:11:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">EconTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>The UFO boom is not an astronomy question, it is a question of belief</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">When the evidence stops at testimony, whether the universe is crowded becomes a matter of faith. The further science advances, the rarer extraterrestrial life looks in the observations; when we talk about UFOs, what we are really talking about is ourselves.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">UFOs</span><span class=\"tag\">Extraterrestrial life</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"The Cost of Banning Kidney Sales: 130,000 New Cases a Year, Fewer Than 30,000 Transplants The US sees about 130,000 new cases of kidney failure a year and performs fewer than 30,000 transplants. Al Roth takes apart where the repugnance toward organ markets comes from, and argues that regulated compensation could save lives without exploitation. The Gray Area with Sean Illing The Gray Area Kidneys for sale Kidney Transplants Organ Sales Market Design Moral Controversy Public Policy repugnant transaction market design non-directed donor\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-grayarea-禁止卖肾的代价-本不该失去的生命/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7c8b1deddea1e240-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">49:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Gray Area with Sean Illing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>The Cost of Banning Kidney Sales: 130,000 New Cases a Year, Fewer Than 30,000 Transplants</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The US sees about 130,000 new cases of kidney failure a year and performs fewer than 30,000 transplants. Al Roth takes apart where the repugnance toward organ markets comes from, and argues that regulated compensation could save lives without exploitation.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Kidney Transplants</span><span class=\"tag\">Organ Sales</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Procrastination isn&#x27;t laziness — your brain has priced the effort too high Neurologist Masud Husain uses case histories and brain imaging to show that procrastination and apathy come from a perceived effort cost that is too high, not from an absence of desire. Lowering the activation energy, planning ahead and breaking tasks into steps are the practical ways around the barrier. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab How to Improve Motivation &amp; Overcome Procrastination | Dr. Masud Husain Motivation Procrastination Dopamine Neuroscience Alzheimer&#x27;s disease Addiction apathy basal ganglia activation energy wanting vs liking\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-huberman-动机不是欲望问题-而是激活能量障碍/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d9e03163dcfb779e-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">2:20:21</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Procrastination isn&#x27;t laziness — your brain has priced the effort too high</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Neurologist Masud Husain uses case histories and brain imaging to show that procrastination and apathy come from a perceived effort cost that is too high, not from an absence of desire. Lowering the activation energy, planning ahead and breaking tasks into steps are the practical ways around the barrier.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Motivation</span><span class=\"tag\">Procrastination</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Africa isn&#x27;t short of resources. It&#x27;s short of population density Dangote built his fortune on a monopoly concession, then spent $20 billion of his own money to build Nigeria&#x27;s refinery and become Africa&#x27;s richest man. But Studwell argues the variable that decides whether Africa can repeat the Asian miracle is the urban market size that comes with population density, not minerals and not aid. Odd Lots Odd Lots The Nigerian Industrial Behemoth That Could Reshape the African Economy African economy industrial policy manufacturing demographics China-Africa relations land reform flying geese paradigm resource curse developmental state kulak demonstration effect\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-oddlots-非洲工业化不缺资源-缺的是人口密度/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">58:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Africa isn&#x27;t short of resources. It&#x27;s short of population density</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Dangote built his fortune on a monopoly concession, then spent $20 billion of his own money to build Nigeria&#x27;s refinery and become Africa&#x27;s richest man. But Studwell argues the variable that decides whether Africa can repeat the Asian miracle is the urban market size that comes with population density, not minerals and not aid.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">African economy</span><span class=\"tag\">industrial policy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Water privatisation is not destiny: Uruguay&#x27;s referendum on the right to water On 31 October 2004, Uruguayans voted 64.7% in favour of writing ‘water is a natural resource essential to life’ into the constitution, and of restricting water supply to the state alone — making Uruguay the first country in the world to lock water privatisation out at constitutional level. The movement began with a tap being shut off in a coastal city. Witness History Witness History Uruguay&#x27;s water referendum Water rights Referendum Privatisation Uruguay Social movements Constitution concession mate\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-witness-供水私有化不是宿命-乌拉圭公投的水权胜利/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4ad7b76f4c635f2c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">10:12</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Water privatisation is not destiny: Uruguay&#x27;s referendum on the right to water</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">On 31 October 2004, Uruguayans voted 64.7% in favour of writing ‘water is a natural resource essential to life’ into the constitution, and of restricting water supply to the state alone — making Uruguay the first country in the world to lock water privatisation out at constitutional level. The movement began with a tap being shut off in a coastal city.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Water rights</span><span class=\"tag\">Referendum</span>\n</div></div></a>"]